<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulu Archipelago</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sulu Archipelago is a chain of islands in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, stretching between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilan">Basilan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawi-tawi">Tawi-Tawi</a>. From the 15th century it was the heart of the Sultanate of Sulu, a Muslim maritime state that traded and raided across the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> struggled to control it, and it remains largely Muslim, inhabited mainly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a>, Sama and Yakan peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulu-archipelago.jpg" alt="Sulu Archipelago" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kleomarlo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulu_archipelago.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sulu Archipelago is a chain of islands in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. It forms the northern limit of the Celebes Sea and the southern limit of the Sulu Sea. Its islands belong to the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilan">Basilan</a>, Sulu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawi-tawi">Tawi-Tawi</a>, whose first syllables give it the name Basulta. Basilan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>, Tawi-Tawi and other islands are extinct volcanic cones rising from the southernmost of several small submarine ridges; the chain is not, as is often supposed, the remains of a land bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and the Philippines. The largest municipalities are on Jolo, and the islands are an important migration route for birds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The archipelago was once part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> mentions it as Solot. It later became part of the Sultanate of Sulu, founded in 1405 by Shari'ful Hashem Syed Abu Bak'r, which also included parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palawan">Palawan</a>. Spanish expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> met firm and organised resistance. In 1578 a Spanish mission to Jolo produced a compromise in which the sultan paid tribute in pearls, but the Spanish burned Jolo in 1587, and attacks in 1598, 1628 and 1630 failed. In 1638 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-hurtado-de-corcuera">Sebastián Hurtado de Corcuera</a> took Jolo with about 80 ships and 2,000 troops, but a garrison there was worn away by raids and was gone by 1645.</p><p>Hostilities resumed in the eighteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> became a new player after occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1762 to 1764, when it made trading alliances with the sultanate. In 1848 and 1850 Spanish forces under Narciso Claveria and Juan Urbiztondo destroyed the Balangingi strongholds, a powerful ally of the sultanate, and its maritime power began to decline. In 1876 Admiral Jose Malcampo captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> with 9,000 soldiers and established a Spanish settlement, though the Spaniards were never secure there. After the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898, the independence movement known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-rebellion">Moro Rebellion</a>, from 1899 to 1913, continued the resistance against the United States, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> became independent on 4 July 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The archipelago is the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> and of various Sama peoples, including the semi-nomadic Bajau, the Yakan and the Jama Mapun. Tausug is widely spoken as a first and second language, Yakan mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilan">Basilan</a>, and many dialects of Sinama from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawi-tawi">Tawi-Tawi</a> to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>. Jolo still has short stretches of its Spanish perimeter wall, partly covered by buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulu Archipelago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States, which had at first signed the Kiram-Bates Treaty of 1899 recognising the Sulu Sultanate, created the Moro Province in 1903 to govern the Muslim regions of Mindanao and the <b>Sulu Archipelago</b>, including the former Lanao sultanates and Maguindanao.</blockquote><a href="/state/moro-province">Moro Province</a></li></ul></section>
